id: k8s.resources.limits
kind: kubernetes
title: Resource limits
aliases: limits, cpu limit, memory limit
tags: resources, reliability, performance
questions:
  - what happens when a container exceeds its memory limit?
  - should you set a CPU limit in production?
invariant: Limits are enforcement: exceeding a memory limit kills the container, exceeding a CPU limit throttles it.
answer_30s: Memory limits are hard (OOMKill), CPU limits throttle rather than kill, and CPU limits are the usual cause of latency nobody can explain.
answer_2m: |
  Two different mechanisms with two different failure shapes. Memory: the
  container is killed with OOMKilled and restarts, so a leak looks like a
  restart loop. CPU: the container is throttled via cgroup quota in 100ms
  periods, so a Go or async service with a burst-y profile shows latency
  spikes and "the CPU is idle" dashboards -- throttling, not saturation, is the
  signal (`container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total`). Setting requests and
  letting CPU burst is a common production policy; memory limits should be well
  above the steady-state working set.
when_to_use:
  - memory limits to bound blast radius; CPU limits only with a deliberate reason
avoid_when:
  - CPU limits on latency-sensitive services without measuring throttling
commands:
  - kubectl top pod => actual usage next to the declared limits
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137
failure_modes:
  - memory limit below the real working set, causing OOMKill restart loops
  - CPU limit causing throttling under burst, misread as a slow dependency
related:
  - k8s.resources.requests
  - design.observability
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/
